::ALG2_U06_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L01_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which factor represents 7% annual growth? {
= 1.07#Correct. A 7% increase uses 1 + 0.07, so the growth factor is 1.07.
~ 0.07#This is the rate only, not the factor that keeps the original amount and adds growth.
~ 0.93#This would represent a 7% decrease because 93% remains.
~ 7#This treats the percent as a whole number instead of converting to a decimal rate.
}

::ALG2_U06_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L01_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A $900 account earns 4% annually. Which model gives the balance after t years? {
~ A=900(0.04)<sup>t</sup>#This uses the rate as the entire factor and loses the original 100%.
= A=900(1.04)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Growth by 4% means the account keeps 100% and gains 4% each year.
~ A=900+4t#This is linear growth by 4 dollars per year, not exponential percent growth.
~ A=4(900)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses the principal and rate and creates the wrong type of model.
}

::ALG2_U06_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L01_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A $15,000 car depreciates by 12% each year. What factor should be used? {
~ 1.12#This would increase the value by 12%, not depreciate it.
~ 0.12#This is the percent lost, not the percent that remains.
= 0.88#Correct. If 12% is lost, then 88% remains, so the factor is 0.88.
~ 12#This does not convert the percent to a decimal factor.
}

::ALG2_U06_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L01_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What does P represent in A=P(1+r)<sup>t</sup>? {
~ the annual rate#The rate is represented by r, not P.
~ the time in years#The exponent t represents time.
~ the final balance#The final amount is represented by A.
= the starting amount#Correct. P is the principal, or starting amount.
}

::ALG2_U06_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L01_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>$2,000 grows by 5% for 2 years. Which expression estimates the final amount? {
= 2000(1.05)<sup>2</sup>#Correct. The principal is 2000, the growth factor is 1.05, and the time is 2 years.
~ 2000(0.05)<sup>2</sup>#This uses only the rate, so it does not preserve the original amount.
~ 2000(0.95)<sup>2</sup>#This models 5% decay, not 5% growth.
~ 2000+5(2)#This is a linear dollar increase and does not represent 5% annual growth.
}
